# The deployed configuration of this fleet: which service does which job, and what
# it is allowed to spend. Sourced by run.sh for every daemon, so all three units share
# one answer and a change is one edit here plus a fleet restart.
#
# This is shell, sourced with `set -a`, so every assignment is exported. Keep it to
# plain KEY=value with no logic — a config file that can branch is a program.
#
# Everything here has a default in fanfic/config.py; this file records the choices
# this machine actually runs with and why they are the ones they are.

# --- Who writes, and who judges ----------------------------------------------
#
# Claude Opus, for every text call in the pipeline: research, planning, outlining,
# drafting, continuation, editing, bible merges, art direction, vision critique. On a
# seat rather than an API key, so it costs allowance rather than money.
#
# This used to be a two-tier split — Sonnet drafted, Opus judged — with a per-role
# override that pushed research down to Sonnet and had already been forced to pull
# art direction back up after the cheap tier spent a run choosing moments no image
# model could draw. Every one of those knobs was a second suspect for any quality
# problem in a project whose entire product is prose quality. There is no volume
# argument that beats that, so there are no tiers.
FANFIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# --- Pictures ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NO API KEY, AND NO BILL. Pictures are drawn by driving gemini.google.com in a real
# signed-in Chrome session (tools/gemini_art.js) rather than by calling the Images
# API on a billed key.
#
# The API path was removed rather than kept as a fallback, and the reason is the art:
# it returned a PNG every time and the pictures were the weakest thing in the finished
# books — samey between renders, flat, often distracting enough that a chapter read
# better with the slot empty. The same model through its own product does not have
# that problem. That is not a prompt difference; it is the difference between a bare
# endpoint and the thing built around it.
#
# Set the profile up ONCE with `scripts/gemini-login.sh`, then never again until
# Google expires the session. The directory below IS the credential.
FANFIC_GEMINI_PROFILE_DIR=/Users/mikeyferguson/.config/fanfic/chrome-gemini

# Headless. Set to 1 to watch a render in a visible window, which is the only way to
# debug a selector Google has moved.
FANFIC_IMAGE_HEADFUL=0

# A CEILING on pictures per chapter, not a count.
#
# A chapter gets one picture per scene segment: the writer marks every change of place
# or time, and each segment is one setting, one moment, one image. A chapter that
# moves four times gets four pictures and one that stays in a room gets one, which is
# the right answer in both cases and is not a number anybody has to choose. This just
# stops that from being unbounded, and it must stay above the number of segments a
# chapter actually has — it was 2 for a while, left over from the era when it really
# was a count, and it silently threw the whole per-segment design away.
FANFIC_IMAGES_PER_CHAPTER=6

# The runaway stop, in RENDERS rather than dollars — there are no dollars any more.
#
# RAISED FROM 800 TO 1600 ON THE FIRST REAL BOOK, on measurement rather than nerves.
# 800 was sized on an assumed ~2 renders per kept picture. The Jedi Knight book is
# running at a 32-42% keep rate, which is ~3 renders per picture: Gemini refuses a
# share of prompts outright (a classifier firing, not a verdict — it will draw the
# identical prompt on a later try), and the vision critic rejects a wrong face. Both
# are working as designed and both spend renders.
#
# At 32% the remaining book needed ~574 renders against 648 left. It would probably
# have fitted, and "probably" is the wrong bet here: hitting the ceiling costs TIME,
# never a picture — the book holds in ILLUSTRATING with every slot queued and waits
# for a person. Doubling the ceiling costs nothing (the pictures are free; only
# wall-clock is spent) and it still bounds a genuine runaway at roughly twice what a
# whole book should ever need.
#
# RAISED AGAIN, 1600 -> 2400, on the afternoon of the 31st, for the same reason one
# rung further along: the keep rate fell to 15% and stayed there. The cause is not
# picture quality — the vision critic rejected ONE render in that window against 52
# classifier refusals — it is that Gemini refuses a rising share of prompts outright,
# and every refusal is billed because `billed_render` counts the attempt before it
# makes it. At 15%, 1600 left ~1314 renders against a ~189-picture need: a margin of
# EIGHT pictures, which is not a margin.
#
# Acted on 67 renders rather than the "few hundred" §6b of the handoff asks for, and
# the asymmetry is the argument. Unused ceiling costs literally nothing. Hitting it
# stops the book dead with every slot queued, waiting for a person — and the owner is
# away, so "waits for a person" could mean a day of an idle fleet. Cheap and early
# beats correct and late when one side of the bet is free.
FANFIC_IMAGE_RENDER_BUDGET=2400

# --- TEMPORARY: reference uploads are turned OFF --------------------------------
#
# *** RESTORE THIS TO 6 THE MOMENT GEMINI ACCEPTS UPLOADS AGAIN. ***
# Check with: scripts/check-browser.sh --live  (render 2 of 3 is the reference path)
#
# Set 2026-09-01 ~01:30Z. Gemini stopped accepting reference uploads at about 20:35Z
# the previous evening. It is total, not a quota on volume: a live check with ONE
# reference fails while the identical prompt with none succeeds.
#
#     live render 1/3: plain prompt                  -> ok, 105554 bytes
#     live render 2/3: same prompt, one reference    -> no image after 420s
#
# A failed upload leaves an errored attachment chip and Gemini keeps the SEND control
# disabled, so the prompt can never leave the composer and the render burns its whole
# deadline (§3 of HANDOFF.md). With uploads attempted, every slot spends 3 x 420s
# failing per ladder rung and only draws once it has fallen to rung 3 — a picture of
# the room with nobody in it. The backlog was 35 slots at roughly one picture per 21
# minutes, all of them peopleless.
#
# With uploads off, renders go through immediately and `render_scene` keeps the
# appearance paragraphs in the prompt (it now checks whether pictures will ACTUALLY be
# attached, not just whether the ladder wants them). That is the prose-anchored
# fallback this project already accepts — "merely imprecise rather than actively
# depicting somebody else" — and it puts the cast back in the frame.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS while it is on: no picture is conditioned on a locked reference
# sheet, which is the answer to hard problem 3. Run `scripts/unanchored-scenes.sh` to
# list every picture drawn this way; redraw the ones that matter once uploads are back
# by deleting the .png and its .retry.
FANFIC_IMAGE_MAX_UPLOADS=0

# --- How long to wait for one picture ----------------------------------------
#
# LEFT AT THE CODE DEFAULT (420s) ON PURPOSE. It was set to 180 here for about three
# hours and that looks like it cost half the illustrator's throughput.
#
# The reasoning for 180 was: 81 successful renders, max 51s, nothing between 51s and the
# deadline, so a render still going at 180s is hung. **The flaw is in what was
# measured.** Those durations were paired from log lines that begin when the PAGE starts
# working — they never included the time to insert a ~2KB prompt and get it submitted.
# A slow send makes the whole driver call long while the render itself is quick.
#
# What happened after the change, as a share of all render attempts that hour:
#
#     hr 07-16 (600s, then 420s)    0-2% ended "no image after Ns" with an empty page
#     hr 17    (420 -> 180)          19%
#     hr 18    (180s)                21%
#     hr 19    (180s)                54%
#     hr 20    (180s)                52%
#
# Those failures are the "prompt never left the composer" signature, and at 180s they
# consumed roughly half of every attempt made. Attempts per hour FELL from 60 to 27
# because each one burns the full deadline.
#
# This is correlation, not proof — hour 17 has two of them at 420s, so the deadline is
# not the whole story. But it is my change, it is cheap to undo, and the hours that ran
# at 420s and 600s show near-zero. If you want to try a shorter deadline again, measure
# the FULL driver call (insert, send, wait) rather than the page's own working time, and
# watch this share rather than the raw count.

# --- Quiet hours -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OFF, by a standing decision rather than a temporary override.
#
# Quiet hours exist because the seat is shared with the person who owns this machine,
# and a fleet drafting through a Monday afternoon spends capacity that person needs.
# That reasoning is real and unchanged — it is simply not the trade the owner wants.
# A full-length book is days of run time; with 09:00-17:00 Central blanked out on
# weekdays it is 9 run-hours a weekday against 17 at the weekend, and the owner would
# rather have the book and feel the contention.
#
# The cost is worth stating plainly, because it is paid by a person rather than by the
# fleet: while a run is in progress, the owner's own use of the same seat will feel
# slower, and an allowance ceiling hit at 3pm is one they hit too. Set this to 1 to get
# the working day back; nothing else has to change, and a run in flight simply pauses
# at the next cycle and resumes at 17:00.
#
# Note that this now gates ONLY the shared Claude seat. Drawing does not touch it —
# the pictures come out of a browser session — but a scene is not accepted until it
# has been critiqued, and critique is Claude, so the illustrator pauses too.
FANFIC_QUIET_HOURS=0
